Supply Chain Resilience: Guide for Water and Wastewater Utilities — EPA (@cs-epa-resilience: 0, is #reference, #part-of @cs-sources) { r_2NVE96BRQ8Q3WYFP2ZQ3NTE11F
US EPA. Supply Chain Resilience: Guide for Water and Wastewater Utilities, September 2022, EPA 810-F-22-007. [Official guide](https://www.epa.gov/waterutilityresponse/supply-chain-resilience-guide-water-and-wastewater-utilities) · [Complete PDF](https://www.epa.gov/system/files/documents/2022-09/220908-SCResiliency_508.pdf).

Read the entire guide and examine each proposed change against an actual chemical user's quantities, grade, storage, staffing and time to approval. Use the case studies to compare backup suppliers, partnerships, inventory and operational flexibility; reconstruct the physical conditions needed for a nominal alternative to become usable.

Official identity, date and scope were checked on 29 October 2026. Its US institutional arrangements are a dated case, not a universal procedure. Government and contributed-component rights are distinct. The reading supports an explicit investigation and continuity plan; it does not establish that the modelled supplier, emergency approval or alternate grade is actually available. No source file is copied.

